**What is claimed is:**

1. **An aircraft energy system**, comprising:  
   an aircraft fuselage;  
   an electrically rechargeable battery configured to supply electrical power to at least one aircraft propulsion motor or aircraft auxiliary load;  
   a plurality of photovoltaic cells mounted on an exterior surface of the aircraft fuselage and electrically couplable to the battery;  
   an external-power interface configured to receive electrical power from an electrical grid while the aircraft is connected to an external power source;  
   a reversible fuel-cell assembly selectively operable in:  
   (i) a power-generation mode in which a fuel and an oxidant are electrochemically reacted to generate electrical power for charging the battery, and  
   (ii) a water-electrolysis mode in which electrical power is applied to water to produce at least hydrogen and oxygen;  
   one or more reactant-storage vessels fluidly coupled to the reversible fuel-cell assembly and configured to store at least the hydrogen produced in the water-electrolysis mode;  
   a power-conversion and distribution circuit electrically coupling the battery, the photovoltaic cells, the external-power interface, and the reversible fuel-cell assembly; and  
   a controller configured to selectively:  
   charge the battery using electrical power generated by the reversible fuel-cell assembly, electrical power generated by the photovoltaic cells, electrical power received through the external-power interface, or a combination thereof; and  
   switch the reversible fuel-cell assembly between the power-generation mode and the water-electrolysis mode based on at least one of a battery state of charge, photovoltaic power availability, external electrical power availability, stored-reactant quantity, aircraft operating state, or predicted aircraft energy demand.

2. **The aircraft energy system of claim 1**, wherein, in the water-electrolysis mode, the controller supplies the reversible fuel-cell assembly with power from the photovoltaic cells, the external-power interface, the battery, or a combination thereof.

3. **The aircraft energy system of claim 1**, wherein water produced during operation in the power-generation mode is collected in a water-storage reservoir and subsequently supplied to the reversible fuel-cell assembly during operation in the water-electrolysis mode.

4. **The aircraft energy system of claim 1**, wherein the one or more reactant-storage vessels comprise a hydrogen-storage vessel and an oxygen-storage vessel, and wherein the hydrogen and oxygen produced in the water-electrolysis mode are stored for subsequent use in the power-generation mode.

5. **The aircraft energy system of claim 1**, wherein the controller prioritizes battery charging from the photovoltaic cells over battery charging from the reversible fuel-cell assembly when photovoltaic power exceeds a threshold.

6. **The aircraft energy system of claim 1**, wherein the controller operates the reversible fuel-cell assembly in the water-electrolysis mode when the battery state of charge exceeds a predetermined level and available photovoltaic or external-grid power exceeds a current aircraft electrical demand.

7. **The aircraft energy system of claim 1**, wherein the controller operates the reversible fuel-cell assembly in the power-generation mode when the battery state of charge falls below a predetermined level, aircraft electrical demand exceeds available photovoltaic power, or the external-power interface is disconnected from the electrical grid.

8. **The aircraft energy system of claim 1**, wherein the power-conversion and distribution circuit comprises one or more bidirectional power converters configured to regulate electrical energy transfer among the battery, the photovoltaic cells, the external-power interface, and the reversible fuel-cell assembly.

9. **The aircraft energy system of claim 1**, further comprising isolation devices configured to electrically isolate the external-power interface during flight and to fluidly isolate the one or more reactant-storage vessels in response to a detected fault.

10. **A method of managing energy aboard an aircraft**, comprising:  
    receiving electrical power from fuselage-mounted photovoltaic cells, an external electrical grid, or both;  
    selectively directing the received electrical power to an aircraft battery;  
    operating a reversible fuel-cell assembly in a water-electrolysis mode to convert water into hydrogen and oxygen using at least a portion of the received electrical power;  
    storing at least the hydrogen produced in the water-electrolysis mode;  
    switching the reversible fuel-cell assembly to a power-generation mode;  
    electrochemically reacting the stored hydrogen with an oxidant to generate electrical power; and  
    directing the generated electrical power to the aircraft battery, an aircraft propulsion system, an aircraft auxiliary load, or a combination thereof.

### Hydrogen-Recycling Aerostatic Flight System

A hydrogen-recycling flight system comprises an aircraft fuselage, at least one variable-volume or selectively inflatable hydrogen-containing balloon mounted to or integrated with the fuselage, a hydrogen fuel-cell power plant, an electrolyzer, respective high-pressure hydrogen and oxygen storage vessels, and an electronic flight-control system operatively coupled thereto.

During operation, hydrogen and oxygen are supplied from the respective storage vessels to the fuel-cell power plant. The fuel-cell power plant electrochemically combines the hydrogen and oxygen to produce electrical power, reaction water, and waste heat. The electrical power may be supplied to propulsion motors, avionics, pumps, compressors, valves, and other aircraft loads. At least a portion of the reaction water is collected in a water-management reservoir and delivered to the electrolyzer. Subject to the availability of electrical energy, the electrolyzer dissociates the collected water into hydrogen and oxygen. The resulting gases are dried, purified, compressed, and returned to their respective high-pressure storage vessels or, in the case of hydrogen, selectively delivered to the balloon.

The balloon provides aerostatic lift by displacing ambient air with hydrogen having a lower density than the surrounding atmosphere. Balloon volume, internal pressure, or both are regulated by a gas-management assembly comprising one or more controllable valves, compressors, regulators, manifolds, and gas-transfer conduits. The flight-control system determines a commanded buoyancy state based on sensed parameters including altitude, ambient pressure, balloon pressure, balloon volume, aircraft mass, vertical velocity, and hydrogen inventory. To increase lift, the system transfers hydrogen from a storage vessel into the balloon, thereby increasing displaced-air volume. To decrease lift, hydrogen is withdrawn from the balloon, compressed, and returned to storage, or is transferred to the fuel-cell supply circuit. Emergency venting may be provided as a safety function, although routine altitude control preferably retains the hydrogen within a substantially closed gas circuit.

The oxygen generated by electrolysis is stored separately from the hydrogen in an oxygen-rated high-pressure vessel and may subsequently be supplied to the fuel cell, particularly where operation is intended to be independent of atmospheric oxygen. Hydrogen and oxygen conduits, tanks, and associated equipment are physically segregated and provided with nonreturn valves, pressure-relief devices, leak detectors, flame arrestors, ventilation paths, and control interlocks configured to prevent unintended mixing or ignition.

The term “hydrogen recycling” denotes recovery of fuel-cell reaction water and regeneration of hydrogen and oxygen by electrolysis. It does not imply net energy production or thermodynamically lossless operation. Because electrolysis, gas purification, compression, storage, and fuel-cell conversion incur energy losses, the system requires an external or onboard energy source—such as solar arrays, regenerative energy capture, or initially stored electrical energy—to sustain electrolysis and auxiliary loads. Accordingly, the electrolyzer functions as an energy-storage and reactant-regeneration subsystem rather than as a source of perpetual propulsion or lift.

In a representative control method, the system:

1. supplies stored hydrogen and oxygen to the fuel cell;
2. converts the reactants into electrical energy and water;
3. collects and conditions the resulting water;
4. electrolyzes the conditioned water using available electrical power;
5. separates, dries, and compresses the generated hydrogen and oxygen;
6. stores the gases in respective pressure vessels;
7. selectively transfers hydrogen between the hydrogen vessel and the fuselage-mounted balloon; and
8. regulates the transfer in closed-loop response to a commanded altitude, buoyancy, or vertical-velocity condition.

Thus configured, the aircraft combines fuel-cell electrical generation, water recovery, electrolysis-based reactant regeneration, and reversible hydrogen-balloon inflation to provide controllable aerostatic lift while minimizing routine loss of lifting gas.
